[%emt 0:00:22] [%think 1299] [%out 1630] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0]
[%cachewrite 3584] Red has a rook and two pawns against my two elephants, so
I'm clearly lost. The practical goal is to hold on as long as possible and aim
for the 200-half-move draw. Legal king moves: - **f8**: the rook on f6 controls
the f-file, so the king cannot go there. - **d8**: legal. - **e9**: legal. The
red advisor on e1 blocks the generals from facing each other, so there's no
flying-general problem. Elephant moves: - **e7 to g5**: the elephant's eye at
f6 is blocked by the red rook, so this is illegal. - **e7 to c5, e7 to g9, c9
to a7**: legal, but they break the connection between the two elephants. The
most solid choice is to keep the elephants linked on c9 and e7 and step the
king back to the base line. - If the rook checks on f9, it has no protection
and the king can take it. - If the rook swings to the d-file and checks, the
king can go back up to e8. } 78. 车四进二 将５平４ { [%emt 0:00:32]
